# rustc 1.100.0-nightly (1303417c4 2026-09-21)
# corpus: /Users/danielstahl/.pi/agent/sessions/--Users-danielstahl-Documents-code-ml-personal-harness-pi-beads-looprs--
#
# The ONLY manifest difference between the fancy blocks and the onig blocks:
#   fancy (shipped): syntect = { version = "5.3.0", default-features = false, features = ["default-fancy"] } # pure-Rust regex
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# THE NUMBERS  (written from the blocks below, not from memory; every figure
# below appears in a block after this point, so the summary can be contradicted)
#
# release profile, Darwin 25.6.0 arm64, one machine, one corpus: 12 real `pi`
# passes → 626 fenced blocks → 5,959 highlighted code lines. Each latency block
# is run twice; where the two samples disagree, both are quoted.
#
#                     the load          warm()      whole-corpus    steady /line    warm RSS
#                     (either backend)              highlight     (rust)
# ------------------  ---------------   ---------   ------------   -------------   -----------
# fancy (shipped)     0.9 ms / 431 KiB  64.1 ms    482.6 ms       87.2 µs       44.80 MiB
# onig (alternative)  0.9 ms / 431 KiB   8.9 ms    153.2 ms       26.7 µs        9.38 MiB
#
# 1. **The load is small, and it was never on the startup path.**
#    `md::highlighter()` cold: 0.9 ms, 430.6 KiB retained on the Rust heap,
#    518.9 KiB peak, **+1.73 MiB of RSS** (3.44 MiB baseline → 5.17 MiB). The
#    SyntaxSet is 75 syntaxes at 0.3 ms / 396.8 KiB peak; the ThemeSet is 7
#    themes at 0.4 ms / 138.9 KiB peak. Both backends read the same dump and
#    measure the same 430.6 KiB, which is the tell that the "megabytes" sentence
#    in `session::view::RETAINED_BYTES_WORST_CASE` was never about the load.
#
# 2. **The real cost is the per-language lazy regex compile, and it lands in a
#    frame mid-stream.** syntect compiles each regex on first use and caches the
#    compiled program inside the `Regex` held by the loaded `SyntaxSet`, so the
#    unit of the stall is the first block of each language, not the first line
#    of the run. fancy, cold: 58.4 ms pooled across the five languages this
#    corpus fences in, largest single first block 20.9 ms = **131% of a 16 ms
#    frame**, worst single line 13.2 ms. fancy, warmed: 5.7 ms pooled, worst
#    line 2.3 ms. Nothing about that stall is attributable to the text being
#    rendered when it arrives, which is why warming — not a cheaper load — is
#    what fixes it.
#
# 3. **The pure-Rust backend is ~3.1× slower over the corpus (3.3× per line) and ~4.8× heavier in
#    RSS.** Warm fancy: 482.6 ms over the corpus, 87.2 µs/line (rust),
#    38.2 KiB churn/line, 44.8 MiB RSS. Warm onig: 153.2 ms, 26.7 µs/line,
#    18.2 KiB churn/line, 9.38 MiB RSS. The RSS gap is the honest one:
#    `CountingAlloc` cannot see a C allocation, so the Rust-heap numbers
#    (26.5 MiB fancy vs 1.0 MiB onig retained after warm) understate the
#    difference and the RSS numbers do not. Fancy stays, on the grounds argued in
#    ADR-0009 §3; the price is written there with these numbers attached.
#
# 4. **Adding a language to the warm list is not free and not huge**: yaml 3.4 ms
#    + 0.9 MiB, javascript 16.3 ms + 6.3 MiB, toml 0 ms + 14 KiB — there is no
#    TOML syntax in the default set, which is worth knowing before anyone tags a
#    fence `toml` and wonders why it is not coloured.
#
# 5. **Warming costs the same in both backends only in wall time.** 64.1 ms of
#    CPU (fancy) buys the disappearance of 52.7 ms of first-block stalls. The
#    exchange is one core for 64 ms at startup, off the draw path, for a stall
#    that would otherwise arrive while a user is reading an answer.
#
# Known measurement limits, stated rather than hidden:
#   * `retained:` lines inside the fence blocks are the *live delta of that
#     counting window*, not the process total — `CountingAlloc::begin()` zeroes
#     its counter, so in the warm run the compiled state is already outside the
#     window. The warm window's own `live heap:` (26.5 MiB fancy / 1.0 MiB onig)
#     and the RSS column are the numbers to quote.
#   * Absolute times move run to run (the very first process to touch the syntax
#     dump pays the page cache; an earlier pass of this same script reported the
#     warm total as 97.5 ms). The ratios held to within 5% across every pass.
#   * RSS for the two backends is not directly comparable at the *load* step
#     with CountingAlloc alone, for the C-allocation reason above. That is why
#     the RSS block runs the test binary directly rather than through `cargo
#     test` — under cargo the answer is cargo's own 87 MiB, every time.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

--- LATENCY (release, real corpus, counting allocator)
# every latency block is run twice. The absolutes in these numbers move by
# up to ~50% run to run on the same machine — the first process to touch
# the syntax dump pays the page cache for everyone after it — while the
# ratios hold. One sample would be quoted as a fact that its own next run
# contradicts; two samples is the spread, visible in the capture.

